/* updates to milestones already on the road, matched by title */
var UPD = {
 peter:{"Martyred in Rome":{
   x:"Peter was executed in Rome under Emperor Nero, probably in the persecution that followed the great fire. His is one of the best-attested deaths of the apostles: Clement of Rome, writing about thirty years later, names Peter among the pillars of the church who were persecuted to death, and Ignatius, Dionysius and Irenaeus agree. Tertullian says he died on a cross, and Origen adds that he asked to be crucified head downward. Jesus had foretold that others would stretch out his hands.",
   src:"1 Clement 5; Ignatius; Dionysius of Corinth; Tertullian; Origen in Eusebius 3.1",
   wit:[["clement","Through jealousy Peter endured not one or two but many labors, and having borne his witness went to his appointed place of glory.","1 Clement 5.4"],
        ["ignatius","I do not give you orders as Peter and Paul did. They were apostles; I am a condemned man.","To the Romans 4.3"],
        ["dionysius","Peter and Paul taught together in Italy and were martyred at the same time.","Eusebius, Church History 2.25.8"],
        ["tertullian","Nero was the first to stain the rising faith with blood; then Peter was girded by another, when he was bound to the cross.","Scorpiace 15"],
        ["origen","At last, having come to Rome, Peter was crucified head downward, for he had asked to suffer in this way.","Eusebius, Church History 3.1.2"]]}},
 paul:{
  "Released to travel again?":{wit:[["clement","Paul reached the limits of the West.","1 Clement 5.7"],["muratorian","Luke leaves out Paul’s journey from Rome to Spain.","lines 38–39"]]},
  "Beheaded in Rome":{
   x:"Paul was executed in Rome under Nero. Clement of Rome, about thirty years later, sums up his life: seven times in chains, driven out and stoned, a preacher in the East and the West who taught righteousness to the whole world and bore witness before rulers. Because Paul was a Roman citizen, later sources say he was beheaded with the sword rather than crucified, traditionally at a place on the road to Ostia.", r:["2 Timothy 4:6-8","2 Timothy 4:16-18"],
   src:"1 Clement 5; Ignatius; Polycarp; Tertullian; Eusebius 2.22 and 2.25",
   wit:[["clement","Seven times in chains, exiled, stoned, a herald in the East and the West, he taught righteousness to the whole world, bore witness before rulers, and so departed.","1 Clement 5.5–7"],
        ["ignatius","You share in the mysteries with Paul, the holy martyr, in whose footsteps I hope to be found.","To the Ephesians 12.2"],
        ["polycarp","Paul and the other apostles are now with the Lord, with whom they also suffered.","To the Philippians 9.1–2"],
        ["tertullian","At Rome Paul won his crown in a death like John the Baptist’s.","Prescription Against Heretics 36"],
        ["eusebius","Under Nero, Paul was beheaded at Rome itself and Peter was crucified.","Church History 2.25.5"]]}},
 john:{
  "Exiled on Patmos":{wit:[["irenaeus","The vision was seen not long ago, almost in our own time, at the end of Domitian’s reign.","Against Heresies 5.30.3"],["eusebius","Under Domitian the apostle John was condemned to live on the island of Patmos.","Church History 3.18.1"]]},
  "The last apostle dies at Ephesus":{
   x:"John died at Ephesus in old age, in the reign of Emperor Trajan (AD 98–117). He is the only one of the Twelve whom the early writers do not call a martyr. Around AD 190 Polycrates, bishop of Ephesus, could still point to his grave.",
   wit:[["irenaeus","John remained with the elders in Asia until the times of Trajan.","Against Heresies 2.22.5; 3.3.4"],
        ["polycrates","John, who leaned on the Lord’s breast, a witness and teacher, sleeps at Ephesus.","Eusebius, Church History 3.31.3; 5.24.3"],
        ["eusebius","John lived on in Asia and governed the churches there.","Church History 3.23.1"]]}},
 andrew:{
  "Preaching in Scythia":{wit:[["origen","Andrew was allotted Scythia.","Eusebius, Church History 3.1.1"]]},
  "Martyred at Patras":{
   x:"The second-century Acts of Andrew say that at Patras in Achaia, Andrew converted Maximilla, the wife of the Roman proconsul Aegeates. The furious governor had him tied, not nailed, to a cross so that he would suffer longer, and Andrew preached to the crowd from the cross until he died. The X-shaped cross linked with his name comes from medieval art.",
   src:"Acts of Andrew (c. AD 150–210); later tradition",
   wit:[["actsandrew","Aegeates ordered Andrew tied to a cross so that he would die slowly.","Martyrdom of Andrew"],
        ["hippolytus","Andrew was crucified at Patrae in Achaia, fastened upright on an olive tree.","On the Twelve"]]}},
 philip:{
  "Ministry in Asia Minor":{wit:[["polycrates","Philip, one of the twelve apostles, sleeps in Hierapolis with two of his daughters who grew old unmarried.","Eusebius, Church History 3.31.3"],["papias","Papias, bishop of Hierapolis, heard remarkable stories from the daughters of Philip.","Eusebius, Church History 3.39.9"]]},
  "Died at Hierapolis":{
   x:"How Philip died is disputed. Heracleon, around AD 170, listed Philip among apostles who did not die confessing Christ before judges, which suggests a natural death. The late Acts of Philip say he was crucified upside down at Hierapolis after the proconsul’s wife became a believer. Pilgrims honored his tomb there for centuries, and archaeologists have excavated his fifth-century shrine.",
   src:"Heracleon; Polycrates; Acts of Philip (4th century); excavations at Hierapolis",
   wit:[["heracleon","Not all the saved confessed with the voice before they departed; among them were Matthew, Philip, Thomas and Levi.","Clement, Stromata 4.9"],
        ["actsphilip","Philip was hung upside down and died at Hierapolis.","Acts of Philip"],
        ["hierapolis","A fifth-century shrine, and a church around a first-century tomb, honor Philip.","Excavations, 1950s–2011"]]}},
 bartholomew:{
  "Matthew’s Gospel carried to “India”":{wit:[["eusebius","Pantaenus found that Bartholomew had preached there and left them Matthew’s Gospel in Hebrew letters.","Church History 5.10.3"],["jerome","Pantaenus found that Bartholomew had preached the coming of the Lord Jesus according to Matthew’s Gospel.","Lives of Illustrious Men 36"]]},
  "Martyred in Armenia":{
   x:"Armenian Christians honor Bartholomew and Thaddaeus as the first to bring the gospel to Armenia. A martyr story from the fifth or sixth century says he converted King Polymius, and the king’s brother Astyages had him beaten, flayed and beheaded at Albanopolis. Another tradition says he was crucified. That is why art shows him holding a knife, or his own skin.",
   src:"Martyrdom of Bartholomew (5th–6th century); Armenian church tradition",
   wit:[["martbart","Astyages had Bartholomew beaten with rods and beheaded.","Martyrdom of Bartholomew"],
        ["hippolytus","Bartholomew was crucified head downward at Allanum, a town of Armenia.","On the Twelve"]]}},
 thomas:{
  "Sent to Parthia":{wit:[["origen","Thomas, as tradition says, was allotted Parthia.","Eusebius, Church History 3.1.1"]]},
  "To India":{d:"c. AD 46–52", y:50,
   x:"The Acts of Thomas say the apostles drew lots and India fell to Thomas. Sold as a builder to a merchant of King Gudnaphar, he was given money to build the king a palace and gave it all to the poor, telling the king his palace was being built in heaven. Gudnaphar was long thought a legend, until coins and a dated inscription showed that an Indo-Parthian king Gondophares really ruled in the north-west of the subcontinent in the AD 20s–40s. The Saint Thomas Christians of Kerala date his arrival on the Malabar coast to AD 52.",
   wit:[["actsthomas","The apostles divided the world by lot, and India fell to Judas Thomas.","Acts of Thomas 1"],["takht","A stone dated in the 26th year of the great king Gudupharasa.","Takht-i-Bahi, AD 46"]]},
  "Martyred near Mylapore":{
   x:"The Acts of Thomas end with King Misdaeus (Mazdai) condemning Thomas for converting his wife and household. Four soldiers led him up a hill and killed him with spears. The Saint Thomas Christians place this at Mylapore, in present-day Chennai, around AD 72. In the fourth century Ephrem the Syrian sang of Thomas’s bones, carried by a merchant from India to Edessa. Heracleon, on the other hand, counted Thomas among apostles who did not die as martyrs.",
   src:"Acts of Thomas (early 3rd century); Ephrem; Saint Thomas Christians",
   wit:[["actsthomas","Four soldiers pierced him with spears, and he died.","Acts of Thomas 168"],
        ["ephrem","A merchant brought the bones of the apostle from India to Edessa.","Hymns of Nisibis 42"],
        ["heracleon","Matthew, Philip, Thomas and Levi did not confess with the voice before they departed.","Clement, Stromata 4.9"]]}},
 matthew:{
  "Preaching to the Hebrews, writing his Gospel":{wit:[["papias","Matthew put the sayings in order in the Hebrew language, and each translated them as best he could.","Eusebius, Church History 3.39.16"],["irenaeus","Matthew published a written Gospel among the Hebrews in their own language, while Peter and Paul were preaching in Rome.","Against Heresies 3.1.1"],["eusebius","Matthew first preached to Hebrews; when about to go to others, he wrote his Gospel in their language.","Church History 3.24.6"]]},
  "Sent to distant peoples":{
   x:"No early writer says how Matthew died. Heracleon, around AD 170, listed Matthew among apostles who did not die confessing Christ before judges, which points to a natural death. Fifth-century historians send him to Ethiopia; others name Persia or Parthia. The medieval Golden Legend says a king named Hirtacus had him killed at the altar. His relics are honored at Salerno in Italy.",
   src:"Heracleon; Rufinus; Socrates Scholasticus; Golden Legend",
   wit:[["heracleon","Matthew, Philip, Thomas and Levi did not confess with the voice before they departed.","Clement, Stromata 4.9"],
        ["rufinus","Matthew was allotted Ethiopia.","Rufinus 10.9; Socrates 1.19"],
        ["golden","Matthew was killed with the sword at the altar on King Hirtacus’s order.","Golden Legend"]]}},
 "james-alphaeus":{"Traditions disagree":{y:63,
   x:"No early record tells where James son of Alphaeus served or how he died. A fourteenth-century Byzantine historian says he was crucified at Ostrakine in Lower Egypt; other late accounts place him in Syria or Persia. Churches that identify him with James the Lord’s brother give him that James’s well-documented death by stoning in Jerusalem.",
   src:"Nikephoros (14th century); later traditions",
   wit:[["nikephoros","James son of Alphaeus was crucified at Ostrakine in Egypt.","Church History"],
        ["hippolytus","James son of Alphaeus was stoned in Jerusalem and buried beside the temple.","On the Twelve"]]}},
 thaddaeus:{
  "Sent to King Abgar of Edessa":{wit:[["eusebius","Thaddaeus, one of the Seventy, was sent to Edessa by Thomas, healed King Abgar and preached there. Eusebius says he translated the story from the Syriac archives.","Church History 1.13"],["addai","Addai preached at Edessa, built a church and died there in peace.","Doctrine of Addai"]]},
  "Martyred with Simon in Persia":{
   x:"A sixth-century Latin collection, Pseudo-Abdias, says Simon and Jude preached in Persia and were killed by pagan priests at a city called Suanir, which is why the Western church remembers them together on 28 October. Armenian tradition places his death at Artaz; another tradition, at Beirut.",
   src:"Pseudo-Abdias (6th century); Armenian church tradition",
   wit:[["abdias","Simon and Jude were killed by the priests of the idols in the city of Suanir.","Passion of Simon and Jude"]]}},
 simon:{
  "Many roads in tradition":{
   x:"Late traditions send Simon to Egypt and North Africa, and even to Britain. Georgian and Abkhazian Christians say he preached on the eastern shore of the Black Sea and died at Nicopsis; a cave near New Athos bears his name. None of these accounts is early.",
   wit:[["dorotheus","Simon the Zealot preached throughout Mauritania and Africa, and was crucified in Britain.","Lists of the apostles"]]},
  "Martyred with Jude in Persia":{
   x:"The most widespread Western tradition, from Pseudo-Abdias in the sixth century, says Simon and Jude Thaddaeus were killed together in Persia. Art shows Simon with a saw, from a legend that he was sawn in half. Some writers confused him with Simeon son of Clopas, second bishop of Jerusalem, whom Hegesippus says was crucified at a great age under Emperor Trajan.",
   src:"Pseudo-Abdias (6th century); later art and tradition",
   wit:[["abdias","Simon and Jude were killed by the priests of the idols in the city of Suanir.","Passion of Simon and Jude"],
        ["hegesippus","Simeon son of Clopas, a relative of the Lord, was crucified at 120 years of age under Trajan.","Eusebius, Church History 3.32"]]}},
 matthias:{"Traditions disagree":{
   x:"Little is known of Matthias after Pentecost. Eusebius counted him among the Seventy, and Clement of Alexandria quotes teaching credited to him. Later traditions disagree: one says he preached in Cappadocia and Colchis on the eastern Black Sea and died there; another, that he was stoned and beheaded in Jerusalem. The Abbey of Saint Matthias at Trier in Germany claims his relics.",
   src:"Eusebius; Clement of Alexandria; later traditions",
   wit:[["clemalex","Matthias taught that we must fight the flesh and make the soul grow through faith and knowledge.","Stromata 3.4"],
        ["eusebius","Matthias, numbered with the apostles in place of Judas, had been one of the Seventy.","Church History 1.12.3"],
        ["nikephoros","Matthias preached in Judea and later in the land by the Caspian, and died there.","Church History"]]}}
};

